592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505722 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f35fe716-2eba-5add-9591-f3a00949c2d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505734 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505734 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505734 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505734 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505734 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1131799e-bf6d-5253-8a7c-8f906c272bb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505734 #edb/ref 1074 "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50}{49}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50-49}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505735 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505735 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505735 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}),$ for $m>0.$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505735 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1203a643-2d1d-5bdd-a410-353b4bd6d719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505735 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16-8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16+8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+|\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m>0,$ we have $\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{m}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505738 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505738 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505738 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡3=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505738 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "151f16ce-dba7-51c2-a380-fc347234ba8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505738 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{9+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505745 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505745 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505745 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505745 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\sqrt{x})$ for $x\\ge 0.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=($\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505745 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21de70c5-f015-584f-8044-af40301c4b75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505745 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain. We have $y\\in [0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the right-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & ={ln}^{2}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require real $y$ and $x<\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ for all $x,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}))}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505748 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505748 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505748 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-3)$ and $x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2480f399-e4b8-57fb-80b2-6dd5de7e6195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505748 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-3).$ Since $x\\ge \\frac{3}{2},$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function, we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2y-3),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,({e}^{2y-3}+{e}^{-(2y-3)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{2y-3}+{e}^{-(2y-3)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{2y-3} & ={e}^{4y-6}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-6}-2x{e}^{2y-3}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{2y-3})^{2}-2x({e}^{2y-3})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{2y-3}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{2y-3} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since $2y-3\\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})+3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505760 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505760 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505760 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505760 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2\\sqrt{3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505760 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e112ff9-57de-5d36-99af-b8be46637221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505760 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2\\sqrt{3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{12-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505763 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505763 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505763 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((x-2)^{2})$ and $x\\ge 2.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f32f1c5-eb40-50cf-b4aa-8b349651b9ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505763 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((x-2)^{2}).$ Since $x\\ge 2,$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function, we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((y-2)^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 2.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,({e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+{e}^{-(y-2)^{2}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+{e}^{-(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{(y-2)^{2}} & ={e}^{2(y-2)^{2}}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(y-2)^{2}}-2x{e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{(y-2)^{2}})^{2}-2x({e}^{(y-2)^{2}})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{(y-2)^{2}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{(y-2)^{2}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want $y\\ge 2,$ select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})}+2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505772 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505772 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505772 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505772 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505772 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5baf8c7a-819a-521e-a261-67b2db30681d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505772 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505778 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505778 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505778 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505778 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $y\\in (-2,2),$ which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505778 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "670d700d-b88f-53f5-a139-73bdf8c93272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505778 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{y}{2})}{1-(-\\frac{y}{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{y}{2}}{1+\\frac{y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{2-y}{2}}{\\frac{2+y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505784 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505784 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505784 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $p\\ge 25,$ express $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b0ffba6-735a-5d4b-89a4-021c8a5b9247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505784 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}+50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}-50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+|\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p\\ge 25,$ we have $\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{p}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505786 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505786 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505786 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $t\\ge 1,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{t}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505786 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "825bc5fd-74d7-5ef1-94a9-eb5175f0a88c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505786 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{t}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+\\sqrt{{t}^{4}-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505789 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505789 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505789 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505789 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83ade4bd-cffc-596f-9566-1eb5e39170cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505789 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{1}{4})}{1-(-\\frac{1}{4})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{1}{4}}{1+\\frac{1}{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}}{\\frac{5}{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505790 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "854f76b3-c5a6-5b21-803f-68a8249b452b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505790 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505792 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505792 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505792 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505792 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505792 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ce8f55a-871b-590a-9437-e579e7e6b7ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505792 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{9}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505793 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505793 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505793 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505793 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡6=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505793 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90b49ff0-e80a-5bed-95c6-624948fee870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505793 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡6 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{{6}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{36-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{35}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505806 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505806 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505806 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505806 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $0<x<\\frac{1}{4},$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505806 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5800963-d7c6-5c24-9f66-ca5ef86b26b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505806 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+4\\sqrt{x}-1}{1-4\\sqrt{x}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{2-4\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505812 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505812 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505812 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505812 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $-2<w<1,$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505812 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c284afac-baa7-5076-92a3-0d89b2214f32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505812 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+\\frac{2w+1}{3}}{1-\\frac{2w+1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3+2w+1}{3}}{\\frac{3-2w-1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4+2w}{2-2w}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+w}{1-w}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505814 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡({x}^{2})$  and $x\\ge 0,$ calculate  ${f}^{-1}(x)$ from first-principles." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2e8309b-0e6c-591c-8179-3fd8b7ab6d9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505814 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $y=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on $x\\ge 0.$ We have $y\\in [0,1)$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,1)$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1}{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1} \\\\ x({e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1) & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1 \\\\ (x-1){e}^{2{y}^{2}} & +(x+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the ${e}^{2{y}^{2}}$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{x+1}{x-1} \\\\ {e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{1+x}{1-x} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive root. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505825 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-3).$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dec2af6f-6d06-5905-b90e-1ab8bd17313c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505825 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-3).$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain, and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2y-3),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2y-3}-{e}^{-(2y-3)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{2y-3}-{e}^{-(2y-3)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{2y-3} & ={e}^{2(2y-3)}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(2y-3)}-2x{e}^{2y-3}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{2y-3})}^{2}-2x({e}^{2y-3})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{2y-3}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{2y-3} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we require real $y,$ we must have ${e}^{2y-3}>0.$ However, $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}<0$ for all real $x.$ Therefore, we select the positive solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505845 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505845 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505845 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505845 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}),$ for $t>0?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505845 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdaedc69-27ce-51b2-8939-51c5e1dbf0b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505845 #edb/ref 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+|\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we have $\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
